<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP is permitted, because this app's backend *is* a plain-HTTP
  server on the local network.

  XSIGHT is a LAN appliance: the kiosk talks to a FastAPI server on the same
  Wi-Fi, reached by IP, with no certificate and no DNS name to issue one for.
  There is no HTTPS endpoint to prefer. (This is the same constraint that forced
  the phone-handoff relay to exist: an HTTPS capture page cannot POST to the
  kiosk's plain-HTTP LAN address, so a cloud relay bridges the two.)

  Why base-config rather than a domain list: the server's address comes from the
  venue's DHCP and changes between sites, and the app finds it by sweeping the
  subnet (XSServerDiscovery). Neither the address nor the range can be enumerated
  here — Android's network security config takes hostnames and literal IPs, not
  CIDR blocks — so an allowlist would make discovery impossible and a LAN backend
  unreachable on any physical device.

  Note that `android:usesCleartextTraffic` in the manifest is IGNORED whenever
  this file is present, so this is the only thing that actually decides the
  policy. With base-config set to false, every request to a LAN IP was blocked by
  the platform before it left the app.

  If this ever gains an internet-facing backend, give that host its own
  domain-config requiring HTTPS rather than relaxing it here.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
